PRETABLE 2 -- #531 ip30abortkill, kill table 2 (27-row instrument, the CLASS CLOSURE)
Written 2026-09-17 BEFORE table 2 runs; this file's own mtime is the order discriminator.
Mandatory: this round EXTENDS a kill-table-backed detector.

================================================================================
WHY THERE IS A SECOND TABLE -- THE #499 AMENDMENT, NOT A THIRD GUESS
================================================================================
TWO CONSECUTIVE PASSES FOUND BREACHES OF ONE CLASS:
  pass 2 (codex)  -- the TICK announce was unobserved post-fix
  pass 2 (opus5)  -- THREE MORE announce cells unobserved, measured at 19/19 each,
                     plus two latch-MECHANISM cells

So the amendment applies: do NOT repair the cell the last mutant used.  Enumerate the whole
space from the function's own inputs, close every cell in ONE diff, and let the closing pass
VERIFY THE ENUMERATION rather than hunt for more cells.  Table 1 already ran and closed five
cells; table 2 exists to test the closure, not to search.

================================================================================
1. THE ENUMERATION, FROM THE FUNCTION'S OWN INPUTS
================================================================================
ANNOUNCE SITES = {0x10020, 0x10028, 0x10030} x {READ, WRITE}, minus 0x10030-READ
(serviced -- it is the one arm that answers correctly and announces nothing), plus TICK.
SIX sites.  Plus the LATCH MECHANISM itself, which is not a site but a shared resource:
its two failure modes are COLLISION (two classes on one bit) and DEFEAT (mask -> 0).

  #  SITE            ROW THAT ATTRIBUTES IT        ADDED BY
  1  0x10020 READ    kill 20 rd txt                pre-existing
  2  0x10028 READ    kill 28 rd txt                pre-existing
  3  0x10030 WRITE   kill 30 txt                   pass 2 (re-bound; was MISBOUND)
  4  TICK            tick 50 txt                   pass 2 (new)
  5  0x10020 WRITE   kill 20 wr txt                *** CLOSURE, new ***
  6  0x10028 WRITE   kill 28 wr txt                *** CLOSURE, new ***
  M1 latch COLLISION xbit isr + xbit tick          *** CLOSURE, new ***
  M2 latch DEFEAT    latch 1x                      *** CLOSURE, new ***
  V  declined VALUE  isr 20 rd val                 *** CLOSURE, new ***

*** THE ATTRIBUTION RULE, which is the whole class and which NO ROW ENCODED BEFORE: ***
All five ISR sites print ONE IDENTICAL STRING behind ONE SHARED BIT.  Therefore a row can
attribute an announce to a SPECIFIC arm only if its session makes a SINGLE ISR access.  A
multi-access session asserts merely "at least one of these arms announced" -- which is exactly
how the old `kill 30 txt' sat green while blind to its own named mutant, and why M9/M5 swept
clean for the measuring seat.  ROWS 5 AND 6 USE SESSIONS THAT WERE ALREADY SINGLE-ACCESS;
they simply never read the transcript.

================================================================================
2. CHANNEL -- per row, the mutant it kills and the observable that differs
================================================================================
  ROW              MUTANT            OBSERVABLE THAT DIFFERS        PREDICTION
  kill 20 wr txt   M7_silent20wr     announce absent vs present     KILL (20/27)
  kill 28 wr txt   M8_silent28wr     announce absent vs present     KILL (20/27)
  xbit isr         M6_sharedlatch    ISR announce COUNT 0 vs 1      KILL (26/27)
  latch 1x         M9_nolatch        ISR announce COUNT 2 vs 1      KILL (26/27)
  isr 20 rd val    M10_mirror20      readback 0x5a5a vs 0x0         KILL (26/27)
  isr clr          M1_del28store     readback 0x5a5a vs 0x5050      KILL -- RE-RUN as a
                                                                    REGRESSION CHECK

*** THE TWO NEW ORACLE SHAPES, AND WHY PRESENCE WAS NOT ENOUGH. ***
`latch 1x' COUNTS rather than detects.  Under M9 the arm announces on EVERY access, so a
presence oracle reads `yes' on the fix AND on the mutant -- the CHANNEL vacuity one level
along.  Only a count varies.  `xbit' puts BOTH announce classes in ONE device lifetime, which
no fresh-process row can do; that is the only shape that can see a bit collision at all.

================================================================================
3. WHY M1_del28store IS RE-RUN -- the #462 lesson, applied rather than cited
================================================================================
#462 measured that ADDING ROWS PERTURBS OTHER MUTANTS' SCORES.  Six rows were just added, so
the five kills table 1 established are claims about an instrument that no longer exists.  M1 is
re-run as the cheapest regression check; if it still reds exactly `isr clr' at 26/27, the added
rows are independent of it.  *A full re-run of all eleven was considered and rejected as cost
without a hypothesis -- the new rows are separate sessions, so the mechanism for perturbation
is absent.  THAT IS A REASONED CLAIM, NOT A MEASURED ONE, AND M1 IS THE ONE TEST OF IT.*

================================================================================
4. THE TABLE-1 PREDICTION THAT LANDED, recorded because the rule asked to be CHECKED
================================================================================
PRETABLE.out predicted, BEFORE table 1 ran, that M6_sharedlatch would ESCAPE ALL 21 ROWS, with
the derivation: every session is a fresh process, so no row exercises both announce classes in
one device lifetime.  **MEASURED: M6_sharedlatch 21/21, CLEAN SWEEP.**

That is the pre-table CHANNEL rule's own claim -- *a row's channel under its mutant is knowable
at WRITING time, before any table runs* -- tested for the first time in this project and HELD.
The first mandated application was told to CHECK the rule rather than merely obey it; this is
the answer, and it is a positive one.

================================================================================
